1
00:00:05,122 --> 00:00:08,616
Take away the observer, and
a second question waits.

2
00:00:08,696 --> 00:00:11,310
Does experience rest on
something beneath it?

3
00:00:12,134 --> 00:00:16,212
Perhaps there is a raw layer,
simply there before any naming,

4
00:00:16,292 --> 00:00:18,295
which concepts then describe.

5
00:00:18,384 --> 00:00:20,265
Philosophers call it the Given.

6
00:00:21,077 --> 00:00:27,077
Locke gave it one shape. Shape and motion,
he said, belong to bodies themselves.

7
00:00:27,245 --> 00:00:32,410
Colour and warmth are only powers in
bodies to produce sensations in us.

8
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:37,668
Later, Wilfrid Sellars argued that a
pure given in experience is a myth.

9
00:00:37,748 --> 00:00:40,960
What we call a sensation
is already conceptual.

10
00:00:41,769 --> 00:00:46,675
Buddhist thought built its own version
centuries earlier, with greater rigour.

11
00:00:46,755 --> 00:00:49,053
Dignāga began with a symmetry.

12
00:00:49,133 --> 00:00:53,469
There are two means of valid
knowledge, perception and inference,

13
00:00:53,549 --> 00:00:55,914
because there are two kinds of object.

14
00:00:55,994 --> 00:00:59,297
The particular, which
characterises itself alone,

15
00:00:59,377 --> 00:01:02,789
is grasped by perception,
free of concepts.

16
00:01:02,869 --> 00:01:06,498
The universal, a character
shared by many things,

17
00:01:06,578 --> 00:01:08,500
is grasped by concept.

18
00:01:09,314 --> 00:01:11,544
Dharmakīrti sharpened the scheme.

19
00:01:11,624 --> 00:01:16,001
The particular is ultimately real
because it alone can do something.

20
00:01:16,081 --> 00:01:20,063
It has causal efficacy, the
power to bring about an effect.

21
00:01:20,143 --> 00:01:21,775
Universals do nothing.

22
00:01:21,855 --> 00:01:24,850
The fire that is merely
thought of burns no one.

23
00:01:25,673 --> 00:01:27,658
So the building has two storeys.

24
00:01:27,738 --> 00:01:33,432
Below are momentary particulars, met by
perception before any concept arrives.

25
00:01:33,512 --> 00:01:37,264
Above are concepts, which
never touch particulars at all.

26
00:01:37,344 --> 00:01:40,896
They are useful, but
strictly they misrepresent.

27
00:01:41,709 --> 00:01:47,458
This is no crude view. Dharmakīrti posits
no bare substance beneath qualities.

28
00:01:47,538 --> 00:01:52,172
The real is what functions, and what
functions is met before any naming.

29
00:01:52,252 --> 00:01:56,293
It is a Given without a substrate,
and the strongest form of it.

30
00:01:57,097 --> 00:02:01,491
Candrakīrti answers Dignāga's
scheme with three moves.

31
00:02:02,322 --> 00:02:05,014
The first concerns the
word characteristic.

32
00:02:05,094 --> 00:02:07,093
Hardness characterises earth.

33
00:02:07,173 --> 00:02:09,634
If the hardness is
different from the earth,

34
00:02:09,714 --> 00:02:12,297
earth apart from hardness has no mark.

35
00:02:12,377 --> 00:02:15,680
If it is the same, nothing
characterises anything.

36
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:19,584
Characteristic and characterised
are designated together:

37
00:02:19,727 --> 00:02:23,560
each is picked out and named
only in relation to the other.

38
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,327
Neither is found first.

39
00:02:25,407 --> 00:02:28,696
A particular singled out
as this, and only this,

40
00:02:28,776 --> 00:02:31,495
has already been singled out by contrast:

41
00:02:31,575 --> 00:02:34,298
the very work reserved
for the upper floor.

42
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:37,734
The second concerns what perception meets.

43
00:02:37,814 --> 00:02:42,150
Candrakīrti keeps perception,
as the world uses it.

44
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:46,905
But what the world perceives is the
pot, not a momentary patch of colour.

45
00:02:46,985 --> 00:02:50,551
A cup on a table is seen
as a cup, from the start.

46
00:02:51,362 --> 00:02:53,850
The third move comes from Nāgārjuna.

47
00:02:53,930 --> 00:02:58,905
Means of knowing and objects known are
each established only in relation to the

48
00:02:58,985 --> 00:03:03,107
other. Each defines the
other, like long and short.

49
00:03:03,942 --> 00:03:07,042
Tsongkhapa makes the conclusion explicit.

50
00:03:07,122 --> 00:03:10,167
Nothing is established by
its own characteristic,

51
00:03:10,247 --> 00:03:11,879
not even conventionally.

52
00:03:11,959 --> 00:03:15,248
Other schools kept the particular
at the level of convention.

53
00:03:15,328 --> 00:03:19,179
The Prāsaṅgika view
refuses it at every level.

54
00:03:19,989 --> 00:03:21,607
The refusal is narrow.

55
00:03:21,687 --> 00:03:23,346
Valid knowing stays.

56
00:03:23,426 --> 00:03:26,824
The snake seen in a rope is
still corrected by a lamp.

57
00:03:26,904 --> 00:03:32,110
What goes is one claim: that perception's
object stands there from its own side,

58
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:34,845
before designation, as a floor.

59
00:03:35,665 --> 00:03:38,927
And efficacy, Dharmakīrti's
deepest reason?

60
00:03:39,007 --> 00:03:42,202
A key's power to open is
found only with the lock,

61
00:03:42,282 --> 00:03:44,049
the hand and the door.

62
00:03:44,129 --> 00:03:46,604
Efficacy is itself a relation.

63
00:03:46,684 --> 00:03:50,639
What looked like the stamp of independent
reality is the clearest mark of

64
00:03:50,719 --> 00:03:51,919
dependence.

65
00:03:52,661 --> 00:03:54,918
So the building has no ground floor.

66
00:03:54,998 --> 00:03:59,252
Perception is not a lower layer,
and concept is not a top coat.

67
00:03:59,332 --> 00:04:03,319
Vividness and designation are
mingled all the way through.

